\f1\fs22 Piotr Dunhilton, CEO of the Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia, charged Gerald Hardman, Commissioner of Ulyssia Police Incorporated and other such commissions as pleased him, with one job:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Locate and open with all due urgency, and with any resources required, the HIBReLabs facility containing the Parramatta Virus.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The security risk of freeing any Professor Hermann or Doctor Anstey from many decades\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Many many decades,\'92 Gerry corrected, and instantly tried to swallow his regret.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who knows what secrets they know, what long-buried dirt they may be able to use as leverage.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s impossible anyone sealed in the labs would still be alive, your Serene Corporateness.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We can\'92t risk any Teenage Mutant Ninja Nerds escaping and spreading the Gummy Bear Virus. Are you aware of the implications?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The entire health industry depends on drip-feeding remedies over decades. An instant cure to anything\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, everything, including ageing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shut up, Hardman.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m agreeing with you, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Shut up especially when you\'92re agreeing with me. You can\'92t lick my arse through a tablet camera, even if I drop my daks and sit on the screen. Me please tell me you understand, the economy needs an underclass to have long, drawn out illnesses and die horribly. And expensively.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I understand, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Good. Now get me that Parramatta Virus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry\'92s screen went blank. \'91Pararetrovirus, your Serene Starch Face,\'92 he bravely said at it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A minute or so later, Gerry received a formal message which, in essence spelled out the consequences of failure in this matter, namely Gerry\'92s instant bankruptcy and his financial footprint being sent in full to the High Tea Lady of the Ulyssian Tax Office.
\f2\i \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry put his extensive team to work. They started with a location assessed as lowest risk: a small facility out in the Western Desert gouged into reasonably soft sedimentary rock where they could experiment with various excavation techniques. They could create any disturbance without anyone credible complaining: any volume of noise or dust; any magnitude of earthquake; any height of sudden artesian geyser. And they could contain any zombie apocalypse of youthful scientists with perfect heads of hair or corns on their feet, should they  emerge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Western Desert facility yielded neither virus, but it did attach a tax-you side-note: in one of the offices they found documentation of another dozen HIBReLabs. Gerry threw caution to the rock saws and diamond-tipped core drills, and ordered the geographically low-risk sites be opened three at a time. As work progressed and he reviewed the equipment in action, he was secretly chuffed to be in command of such resources and budget.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But fear and doubt gnawed at Gerry. The Murphy\'92s Law ganglia in his brain already knew which facility would house the virus, and was constantly nagging him with the knowledge. Its location was under some of the most expensive Airborne towers in the city whose tentacle pylons reached through the Layer and sought out ground support in the inner city area known as the Pale, the poorest district in the city, where geographical and meteorological forces made the Layer especially thick and obnoxious. Blowing shit up under those towers would be tricky, if not impossible. So Gerry continued to hedge his bets by cracking open the easier installations first.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which is not to say cracking those open was easy. A pattern was emerging. All the facilities were at least fifty metres down, under mostly undisturbed volcanic rock or sedimentary conglomerates, domed by at least ten metres of ultra-high-performance concrete composed of cement, microsilica, very fine aggregates, and a mixture of steel and synthetic fibre which served to distribute stress. The fibres introduced a matrix of microscopic cracks that served to prevent larger cracks developing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The work was arduous, and the deeper the progress the more care needed to be taken. They couldn\'92t afford a cave-in, the offices buried underneath needed to remain intact so investigators could search for files which might indicate where the virus might be contained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Over the next weeks, teams opened and explored almost all the labs. Along the way, evidence of more facilities presented. Gerry stepped up the application of resources until only two facilities remained. One was the facility in the Pale that Gerry had been avoiding. The other was a facility obliquely referenced in documents the teams found late in schedule. There was some argument whether the location would house a HIBReLabs facility, since the files in which it was mentioned were always found in the accounting offices of other facilities. That there might be virus kitchens there at all was almost unthinkable, since its address placed it in or under Jamison Tower in the centre of the financial district.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was time for Gerry to confront his nagging fears that the location in the Pale housed the virus and, perhaps, centuries-old living nerd fossils.\

